**Divisibility by 5:** Among the factors, n²+3 ≡ n²+3 (mod 5). Squares mod 5 are {0,1,4}, so n²+3 ∈ {3,4,2} — never 0. So 5 | P iff 5 divides one of (n-2),(n-1),(n+1),(n+2), i.e., **n ≢ 0 (mod 5)**.

**Divisibility by 9:** 
- If n ≡ 0 (mod 3): none of (n±1),(n±2) is divisible by 3; only n²+3 = 3(3k²+1) contributes exactly one factor of 3. So v₃(P) = 1. **Fails.**
- If n ≡ 1 (mod 3): both (n-1) and (n+2) are divisible by 3, giving v₃ ≥ 2. **Passes.**
- If n ≡ 2 (mod 3): both (n-2) and (n+1) are divisible by 3, giving v₃ ≥ 2. **Passes.**

So 9 | P iff **3 ∤ n**.

